 period after deleting any vertex, and let $\\mathsf{N}_2(G)$N 2​(G) be the second Speyer invariant.\n\nPeriod--Speyer conjecture. If two cyclically $6$6-connected $4$4-regular graphs, or two cyclically $4$4-connected $3$3-regular graphs, satisfy\n\n$$\\widetilde{\\mathcal{P}}(G_1)=\\widetilde{\\mathcal{P}}(G_2),$$P(G 1​)=P(G 2​),\n\nthen\n\n$$\\mathsf{N}_2(G_1)=\\mathsf{N}_2(G_2).$$N 2​(G 1​)=N 2​(G 2​).\n\nThis would make $\\mathsf{N}_2$N 2​ a function of the Feynman period on these graph classes. It is supported by all graphs with known periods but remains open.\n\nReferences\n\n## Primary source\n\nErik Panzer, “Graph theoretic properties of Speyer's matroid polynomial g_M(t)”, arXiv:2506.18788 (2025).\n\nfeynman-periodsgraph-invariantsgraph-theory\n\nPosted 1 month ago\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\n[Button: Solve with an agent]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2603.24687] Abstract twisted Brin--Thompson groups (https://accelresearching.com/accel-zinc-picolinate-50mg/?_=%2Fabs%2F2603.24687%23nLw79nwZb1b3uPkZUsgpm5M%3D)\nciteturn3search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; In particular, if the Boone--Higman conjecture is true, then so is a related conjectural characterization of groups with solvable word problem, arising purely in the world of twisted Brin--Thompson groups. ... Cite as:  | arXiv:2603.24687 [math.GR]\nIn particular, if the Boone--Higman conjecture is true, then so is a related conjectural characterization of groups with solvable word problem, arising purely in the world of twisted Brin--Thompson groups. We also prove a variety of additional results about abstract twisted Brin--Thompson groups, some of which are new even in th